 and dr wright which step  tends to have the most user errors  again an excellent question typically we  see the most challenges during specimen  handling and transfer steps  this is because we are typically using  gold grids which are extremely fragile  and inexperienced users may  inadvertently damage the grid while  they're handling the  grid with tweezers and and other  you know manipulators so this is one  reason why many research groups and  vendors are developing workflows that  minimize the steps where individual  grids  i.e and isolation are handled and  they're doing this by preloading the  grid into some sort of stabilizing unit  or or even more so within a chuck  type system that then can be transferred  between one microscope to the next  thank you for the question  thank you very much and actually dr  right i'll give you another one what  cell thickness is appropriate for the  cells on the grid  sure this is a question we get uh  frequently  um so we have had a great success for  culturing cells of all thicknesses on em  grids and i should say we are either  culturing cells  or we're laying down cells on the grids  that are separate from one another so we  are not  at this point in time growing a  confluent monolayer  we still want the cells to have  separation between one another so that  we also have exposed areas of the grid  showing  especially if we're doing correlative  microscopy so we can have those points  of correlation  in addition when we are some of our  experiments  we're looking at cell peripheries so we  want to have those  regions exposed so generally  mammalian or human cells are on the  order of 2 to 15 microns at their  maximum z dimension  these are cells we're culturing on grids  you know and this would be around the  nucleus  the points to consider here are will you  have complete vitrification of the cells  on the grid if you're using plunge  freezing  and when this is a concern one may want  to use a cryoprotectant in the media  prior to freezing  or if the region of interest for your  project is really in the thickest  portion of the cell  you may want to consider suctioning a  cryo-sectioning or a cryo-milling  technique  prior to the cryo-tem imaging or  if your sample is quite thick where you  would not get  complete vitrification using plunge  freezing method  you may want to consider high pressure  freezing  freeze substitution and more  conventional tem imaging  thank you so much dr wright and this  next question is for you dr yang and  it's a two-part question  how many fluorophores can we image with  the cyroclem and how long does it take  to acquire  a tile scan of the whole grid  great question so right now the floral  force we  applied we applied like a two  types of the chloroforms depends on our  experiments we apply  either 100 nanometers or 500 nanometers  uh 100 nanometers within or like 200  nanometers  if we don't want to use a 500 nanometers  uh 100 nanometers we use it for  the localization accuracy estimation and  correlation  and the 5 and 200 nanometers will also  use that to  help us do the correlation and for how  long it takes to get a  uh prior clean this process uh the  longest time take is actually for the  scanning of the tile  or the entire grid and then generally it  takes 40 minutes 30 to 40 minutes  it also depends on the z stacks  you choose to collect on each tile and  then we generally for cells we use 15  micron  and then also depends on the uh the  steps  you use we use the nyquist which is 350  nanometers so you can actually get a  better image for  later deconvolution processing  thank you for that and dr cyber what  kind of  shape geometry can be patterned on the  grid  so a great question any shape or pattern  that you can think of  can really be used during the micro  patterning the minimum  size is going to be determined by the  resolution of your  particular device the the primo system  has a resolution of less than two  microns when you're using a 20x  objective and  we have routinely done patterns with  features as small as 5 microns  one thing you want to think about though  is you probably want your cells  on the carbon support in between the  grid bars  so you will want to design a pattern  that fits within those  single grid squares which are around 90  microns  and thank you and dr yang i'm going to  ask you a question because before i do i  want to thank our audience  because these are just great questions  and we have so many coming in  thank you so much dr ying what is the  time requirement for learning to be  proficient at each stage  of this workflow  great question i think it's really  depends on  the comfort levels of the users  generally i think right now with the  clean system leica system is it's much  easier because the  software layout is very clear i think  the majority of the time  a user have been spending on would be  the sample preparation  as well as a loading process that may  take some  practice and then it's generally take uh  probably ten like five to ten rounds to  get very comfortable  and thank you dr wright this next  question is for you  um first the audience member says hi  this is joanne from  clint nano medicine and how to cut the  cyril block  into slides for em without typical em  fixation and thank you for a  presentation she says  sure joanne that's an excellent question  and so it really depends  on your particular sample that you're  imaging so in our case  we're doing cells that are cultured and  less than a monolayer  and so one of the techniques that has  become prominent today and remember  these are cryofix specimens  people are using cryofib sim so that's a  under cryo condition so better than  minus  150 degrees celsius you're inserting  that into a scanning electron microscope  that has a secondary beam that's the  focused ion beam  and so you can use that to mill down  your specimen  and so there's three major techniques  that can be used that i highlighted on  on one of the slides you can either  produce a lamella  you can do uh just sectioning off the  top and continue imaging  or you can cut out what's termed a list  out  and then transfer that to your grid  and then image just that lift out  portion and so there's a recent paper  by another group in the field and we're  happy to share  uh references to the literature uh that  has shown  using this technique uh on c elegans  one can also you do cryoultramicrotomy  to section material and senate for  cryo-em work if you needed to do  fixation because that was one of your  critical points you have the prospect of  doing  fixation or cryopreservation and then  subsequent free substitution  and embedding if your sample is way too  thick to consider doing any cryo milling  or sectioning approach  thank you again for your question  thank you and this next question is for  you dr seibert  um this is can this technique  be used to track gpcr  internalization  yeah uh i don't have that much  experience  with the gpcr internalization myself  but if it's something that you can  track with fluorescence and  electron microscopy then this technique  should be  applicable to that as well  thank you so much this next question  actually is another question for you dr  seibert and we have time for a couple  more questions  the audience member says i read an  article  on cryo e m structure of the 2019 encode  spike in the profusion confirmation of  rap um at l it's interesting that you've  studied the hiv  chemokine receptors and rsv viruses  how feasible is the application of the  microscopy  in the study of the pathogenesis of  stars cove to  virus  yeah that's a great question and  actually it's  very applicable to the study of the sars  kovi  to virus similar to the other studies  that he mentioned  i think many groups are already using  cryo-em  to study the sars kobe 2 and there's  already several  cryo-em structures that have published  and this is an exciting use of this  technique  to learn more about that virus  thank you for that our next question is  for you  dr wang can the techniques be used  to track gpcr internalization  uh um  the gpcr internalization  yeah i'm not very familiarized with the  size of that  complex but i think that is probably  really small  i guess right now the most success  people get  is actually from the 50 to 100  nanometers  they can use to track it i think it's  doable  under the fluorescent conditioning it's  going to be probably challenging  to localize them very precisely just  due to the size of the complex  thank you so much this next question is  for you  dr cyber i'm trying to find it i just  had it um  actually let me just ask this next  question  um and i'll open it up for whichever one  of you would like to answer it  do you require binoculars on the plunge  freezers for freezing adherent cells or  suspension of cells  on grids using leica gp or  other plunge freezers and or do you  or excuse me or do other plunge freezers  work too  so this is liz i'm happy to take that  question  so on our leica emgp we do have the  binocular feature uh incorporated into  it  and so this is really useful when we're  training new users  on how to use the the unit so that they  can  see how their grid is being manipulated  and how the filter paper is coming in  and blotting it and so they can really  dial in the conditions that we're  interested in for for manipulating the  sample we don't always use binoculars  for  visualizing the grids during the process  either on the emgp  or other systems that we have in the lab  it's really  you know training new people starting  with a new sample  and then once you have everything dialed  in then you can move on to not  necessarily checking it every time  thank you very much and dr cyber this is  our last question  for today regarding the micro patterning  process  are there some limitations with the  patterns  yeah thanks that's a another great  question  um not really so again any  any shape or pattern that you can make  in a illustrator or a computer software  program  can then be projected onto the grid  and with the primo system it can even do  different levels of patterning so  you can have areas of your pattern with  more  ecm adhered than other areas of your  pattern  so it's really powerful flexible  technique and again i'd say the only  limitation  is going to be the the minimum  resolution which is  around 2 microns  thank you so much dr cyber and i want to  thank all three of you dr wright dr  seibert dr yang thank you very much for  your presentation today  do you have any final comments for our  audience before we close  hi sarah thank you for asking that well  i think our final comment would be just  reach out to your vendors your local em  experts and and others in the community  and ask questions like you've asked us  today we're sorry we couldn't address  all of them  but we will happily address them offline  and we will  it's just you have to try there's no  limit to the technologies and being able  to use  em cryo-em and light microscopy to  resolve a variety of different  biological questions  oh and stay tuned for our next webinar  in the series  which is really a virtual hands-on  webinar  where we will show you some of the steps  of how we actually  manipulate cells on the grids and use  micro patterning  and walk you through the steps of the  process so that  hopefully we're providing you with some  confidence that you can go out and  test drive these experimental techniques  on your system of interest  we really appreciate your time today  and we appreciate your time thank you  again dr wright dr seibert dr yang for  your today  and for your contributions to cell  biology and i also like to thank  lab roots and our sponsors leica  microsystems and naviol for underwriting  today's educational webcast  before we go i want to remind the  audience thank you for joining us today  and for interesting questions and  questions we didn't have time for today  and those submitted during the on-demand  period will be addressed by our speaker  via contact information  be provided at the time of registration  and this webcast can be viewed on demand  lab roots will alert you via email when  it's available for replay  we encourage you to share this email  with your colleagues who may have missed  today's  live event until next time take care  be safe bye


